# No Callback Props — Colocate Handlers

Do not pass callback functions (click handlers, submit handlers, change handlers, etc.) down through component props. Define handlers in the lowest component that needs them.

- Reduces prop drilling and coupling between components
- Makes components more self-contained and easier to reason about
- Keeps behavior colocated with the UI that triggers it

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — handler passed from parent
function Parent() {
  const handleClick = () => doSomething();
  return <ChildButton onClick={handleClick} />;
}

function ChildButton({ onClick }: { onClick: () => void }) {
  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={onClick}>
      Click
    </button>
  );
}

// ✅ GOOD — handler defined in the component that uses it
function ChildButton() {
  const handleClick = () => doSomething();
  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={handleClick}>
      Click
    </button>
  );
}
```

If shared **state** is needed between siblings, use feature-scoped context or Jotai — not callback props. Do not centralize every mutation in one parent hook; see [`modular-components.mdc`](modular-components.mdc) for complex dialogs and multi-section UI.
